name: cameragr-product-decisions
description: "Product decisions the user made for the cameragr film-simulation camera app (scope, UI model, storage, filter sourcing)"
metadata:
  node_type: memory
  type: project
  originSessionId: a7b155bd-1e03-4921-b49c-c714f27f4dc7
  modified: 2026-11-18T18:31:54.641Z
---

cameragr is an iOS film-simulation camera app, intended for the App Store. Decisions the user made in planning on 2026-11-17:

- iOS 18+, photos only (no video) in v1; user has a paid Apple Developer account.
- WYSIWYG: the filter is applied live in the viewfinder.
- Filters ("cameras" and "films" are peers, each is just one filter). v1 started as film stocks only; on 2026-11-20 the user approved adding fitted digital-camera looks ("都可以，接进 app 吧，然后去做 CCD"), so GR Neg and CCD ship in v1 (presented as canisters too).
- Filter colour source (decided 2026-11-18 after side-by-side tests): Spectral Film LUT (github.com/JanLohse/spectral_film_lut, MIT), a datasheet-based negative+print spectral simulation; the user found it clearly better. Rejected: the G'MIC-derived Film-Luts collection (all LUTs only 13^3, flat results) and Film-Camera-IOS's procedural grain (looked dirty). Test tooling lives in tools/ and lut-test/ (git-ignored; venv at lut-test/venv-sfl).
- UI follows FIMO's model: one shared skeuomorphic camera body per film category, film swapped via an open-the-back flow on the right third of the screen; each filter gets its own canister art, not its own body.
- Camera body art (chosen 2026-11-18): the "1980s plastic point-and-shoot" concept GPT generated - dark grey textured plastic, ribbed grips both sides, viewfinder hump hiding the front-camera hole top-left, yellow EV slider, LCD strip, film window, orange-red shutter, yellow/red stripe along the bottom. The user wants physical-object realism, rejected flat "instrument panel" styles and a GR IV lookalike, and judged this better than the competing app inFi. Design for the iPhone Duo outer screen first (678x466pt landscape; top corners ~64pt radius, bottom ~6pt; on the user's real Duo the front-camera cutout is a vertical pill, about x 29-66pt, y 26-88pt in landscape, measured from two device screenshots on 2026-11-18; the simulator draws only a circle at 47.7,47.7pt radius 18.5pt - trust the real device. The true screen outline is the simulator's framebufferMask PDF in /Library/Developer/CoreSimulator/Profiles/DeviceTypes/iPhone Duo.simdevicetype; a GPT template made from it is design/mockups/duo_outer_template.png). Reference images live in design/reference/ (git-ignored).
- Widgets (small/medium/large shelf of canisters) deep-link straight into shooting with a chosen filter. No frame counter / roll-limit simulation.
- Import a photo from the system library and apply a filter.
- Output saved to an app-named album in the system Photos library. The unfiltered iPhone original is kept only if the user turns on the Settings switch "also save iPhone original" (user kept the switch, 2026-11-22; replaces the earlier "no original kept").
- GitHub repo: 1skill/cameragr (public).
- App name: **Koco** (changed 2026-11-19; the user pasted a naming recommendation with no words of their own, which I took as their decision). App Store title "Koco Film", subtitle "Retro Analog Camera", home-screen name "Koco"; also the Photos album name and the engraved nameplate. Say the full "Koco Film" to tell it apart from "Koco Widgets" and the similar-sounding "Coco Editor". Title availability still has to be confirmed when the record is created in App Store Connect. Previous name was Peli (2026-11-18): code identifiers still use it (targets PeliWidgets/PeliCapture, URL scheme `peli://`, Swift type names); repo, Xcode project and bundle id stay `cameragr`.
- Primary target device is the iPhone Duo (foldable; the user's own phone, said 2026-11-18). Develop and check layouts on the iPhone Duo simulator first, other iPhones second.

**Why:** FIMO is no longer updated and the user wants its shooting experience with Dazz-quality filters.
**How to apply:** Treat these as settled; don't re-ask. See [[cameragr-working-preferences]].
- Sample photos for look explanation pages are AI-generated with the user's Codex subscription (decided 2026-11-20), not stock: describe scenes in words, never imitate a specific photo; prompt for neutral straight-out-of-camera colour; label pages "示例原图由 AI 生成，效果为 App 实际渲染". The user also wants the base photos to have real artistry (light, composition, moment, styling — like a professional's work, not a casual snapshot); keep colour neutral and exposure normal so the look still shows. Base images must look like unedited iPhone Camera-app output (Smart HDR, auto WB, Standard style) so the film look contrasts strongly — shared clause in tools/sample_prompt_iphone_clause.txt.
- Monetisation and settings (decided 2026-11-20): free download + a few free films, every paid film has a short free trial, single film ¥12 / all films lifetime ¥68 (incl. future films), Family Sharing on, no weekly subs, no ads, no rewards for ratings (App Store 3.2.2/5.6). The film store is the core paid surface: keep it flat and smooth (open chamber → film store → detail at most; buy straight from the card). Settings is a small info page: membership/purchase status, restore purchases, a few switches, about/licences. The user does NOT want a date stamp. China storefront needs an APP备案 (ICP filing) — the user may rent a cheap server for it; identity steps are theirs.
- Multilingual (user, 2026-11-20): every user-facing string must be localisable — English is the base/fallback, Simplified Chinese translated (String Catalogs in Shared/, film_notes.json per language). Never hard-code Chinese UI text again. Plan to add Traditional Chinese, Japanese, Korean; App Store metadata and the website localised too.
- Open-flat inner screen (decided 2026-11-22): the opened camera bag, side by side — held like a book, LEFT = rolls under straps with status tags, RIGHT = card about the roll + photo sleeve (own shots, or samples). It is also the store (tags/card try, buy, choose). The user rejected top/bottom splits and designs that waste space on props. Closing loads the chosen roll (fully shut only).

- Decided 2026-11-22 on the decision page: copyright line = the user's personal name (in project/QUEUE.md); no Kodak/Fuji brand names in keywords; share-video entry in photo review; old store/settings/detail pages redone skeuomorphic now; "try with my photo" can save full-size; App Store screenshots redone for the Duo showing every selling point (not the slab-phone mock); non-fold iPhones zoom the body to fill; half-fold finder = large reflex viewing window; store copy also in zh-Hant/ja/ko; all seven new films (Velvia 50, FP-100C, Double-X, Vista 100, Ektachrome 100D, Natura 1600, Pro 400H); competitor recommendations accepted (no competitor installs).
- Community screen (user, 2026-11-23): reuse the Films page of the open bag. RIGHT = the photo-sleeve album (now others' photos), fuller, scrolling up and down like a feed; LEFT = navigation / categories (prompts, filters) instead of the rolls. It must feel browsable like a photo community, not a wall of a few prints or a private collection. Design this screen first, then make the screenshot from it. Don't invent unrelated objects (check-in books etc.) for this page.
- Inner-screen navigation (user, 2026-11-23): no more leather tabs on the right edge of the open bag — the right page is narrow and is for content (community later). Navigation goes at the bottom of the LEFT page (the roll list above scrolls). Use an element that is physically fixed inside a bag's felt lining; the leather tabs never convinced the user. Options drawn: brass slide-switch plate / sewn woven labels with snaps / elastic loop with enamel tokens (design/nav/).
- Community screen details (user, 2026-11-23, on concept v2): the right side is a photo album — only photos, never a banner row or anything breaking it; everything else (prompt, tabs, search, film filter, me/streak) goes on the LEFT. The left is precious: skeuomorphic but lean, drop decorative bits (e.g. a "Koco 社区" paper title); a slim search bar is fine. Any piece that joins the wide sleeve sheet to the bag must make physical sense (holes that are used, no unexplained clips).
- Navigation chosen (user, 2026-11-23): the radio band window — an arched glass window over a cream dial card, a red pointer stopping on the page's word, moved by a small knurled knob — at the bottom of the LEFT page (design/nav/nav_sliders.png, row 2). Never put text in screenshot art that only one storefront can read (e.g. the Camera Control cue: glow only, no label).
